- dom-getnode-bypasses-element-cache: in `qjs-modules/lib/dom.js`, `GetNode()`
  (used by every `NodeList`/`HTMLCollection` traversal, and therefore by
  `Element.children`, `querySelector`, `querySelectorAll`, etc.) does not
  reuse the `Element.cache` WeakMap that `Document.createElement` uses.
  `Factory.for(owner)` returns a callable `fn`, and `GetNode` looks up the
  constructor via `factory(type)`, i.e. `fn(type)` = `fn[TypeName(type)]?.new`
  — the bare `.new` function. That function has no `.cache` property (the
  memoizing `cache` method lives on the sibling wrapper object `fn[name]`,
  not on `fn[name].new`), so `GetNode`'s `ctor.cache ? ctor.cache(...) :
  ctor(...)` always falls through to `ctor(raw, owner)`, constructing a new
  `Element` instance every time — even for the exact same underlying raw
  node. Found while building `qjs-nanovg/canvas2d.js`: a `<canvas>` element
  created via `document.createElement('canvas')` and later re-fetched via
  `document.getElementById('canvas')` or `document.querySelector('canvas')`
  compared unequal (`!==`) despite `Node.raw()` being identical for both,
  silently dropping any properties (event listeners, custom accessors, etc.)
  attached to the original instance.

    import { Factory } from 'dom';
    const factory = new Factory();
    const document = factory.Document.new({ tagName: '#document', attributes: {}, children: [] }, factory);
    const body = document.createElement('body');
    document.appendChild(body);
    const el = document.createElement('div');
    body.appendChild(el);
    el.myFlag = true;
    const found = [...body.children][0];
    console.log(found === el, found.myFlag); // expected: true true — actual: false undefined

